=0A=0AHi ${first_name},=0A=0AAI can generate an answer before the organizat=
ion has agreed on the process that=0Amakes the answer useful. That gap is w=
here most AI rollouts stall: not on the=0Amodel, but on work that has never=
 been mapped, decided, or owned in writing.=0A=0AThis issue is about closin=
g that gap: why process mapping is quietly becoming=0Apart of the infrastru=
cture reliable AI depends on, what three operators told us=0Aon the floor a=
bout where AI should and should not sit, and a working template=0Afor turni=
ng a flowchart into a decision-ready map.=0A=0ABill=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE SH=
IFT=0A=0A=0AProcess Maps Are Becoming AI Infrastructure.=0A=0AThe model is =
not the constraint. The undocumented process is.=0A=0AMost AI conversations=
 start with a model, a vendor, or a use case. The operators=0Amoving fastes=
t right now are starting one step earlier: is the underlying=0Aprocess clea=
r enough for a model to support it without amplifying the confusion=0Aalrea=
dy built into it=3F=0A=0AThe hidden problem is not simply bad data. It is w=
ork that exists as tribal=0Aknowledge spread across people, spreadsheets, s=
ide systems, and local=0Aworkarounds. Two experienced employees can walk th=
e same process and describe=0Atwo different versions of it, and both believ=
e they are following the same one.=0APut an AI tool on top of that environm=
ent and you get faster output, not shared=0Ajudgment.=0A=0AThe adoption gap=
, by the numbers=0A=0A=C2=B7 34% of operations are already AI-augmented, bu=
t only 43% of collected data is=0Aused effectively, per Rockwell Automation=
's 2026 State of Smart Manufacturing=0Asurvey of 1,560 manufacturing respon=
dents across 17 countries. Read this as a=0Ascale signal, not a benchmark: =
the study is vendor-sponsored and spans companies=0Afrom $100 million to mo=
re than $30 billion in revenue (Rockwell Automation=0A[https://www.rockwell=
automation.com/en-dk/company/news/press-releases/90-of-Manufacturers-Say-Di=
gital-Transformation-Is-Now-Essential-According-to-New-Global-Study.html],=
=0AMay 19, 2026).=0A=0A=C2=B7 The hard deployment problems are operational,=
 not algorithmic. NIST's 2026=0Aroadmap for AI in smart manufacturing names=
 effective data management,=0Aintegration with heterogeneous sensing and co=
ntrol systems, and trustworthy,=0Aexplainable operation in high-stakes envi=
ronments as the critical challenges=0A(NIST, 2026 Roadmap=0A[https://www.ni=
st.gov/publications/2026-roadmap-artificial-intelligence-and-machine-learni=
ng-smart-manufacturing]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 Industrial AI only has meaning inside=
 a defined system and user context.=0ANIST's Industrial AI Management and M=
etrology program says an AI application=0Ashould fulfill an explicit system=
 need and stay bounded by the system's=0Acapabilities, and flags decision, =
planning, and control work as needing clearer=0Astandard operating procedur=
es (NIST Industrial AI Management and Metrology=0A[https://www.nist.gov/pro=
grams-projects/industrial-artificial-intelligence-management-and-metrology-=
iaimm]).=0A=0A=C2=B7 The operating context has to be documented before perf=
ormance can be judged.=0ANIST's AI Risk Management Framework calls for team=
s to define business value,=0Ausers, system requirements, the specific task=
, model limits, human oversight,=0Aand deployment scope, with domain expert=
s informing evaluation in the real=0Aoperating context (NIST AI RMF Core=0A=
[https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/]).=0A=0AThe shift =
is from mapping activities to mapping decisions. The next useful=0Aprocess =
map shows which record is authoritative, what evidence a person needs,=0Awh=
ich exception changes the route, who owns the decision, what happens when t=
he=0Asystem is unavailable, and where AI is allowed to assist.=0A=0AIf two =
experienced employees disagree about the source of truth, the exception=0Ao=
wner, or the evidence of completion, the workflow is not ready for AI.=0A=
=0ABefore an operator asks what AI can automate, the team has to answer a m=
ore=0Abasic question: have we agreed on how the work actually moves=3F=0A=
=0ASources: Rockwell Automation, May 2026; NIST 2026 Roadmap on AI and Mach=
ine=0ALearning for Smart Manufacturing; NIST Industrial AI Management and M=
etrology;=0ANIST AI Risk Management Framework Core.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AFROM T=
HE FLOOR=0A=0A=0AHave We Mapped the Basics=3F=0A=0ALouis Balla, CRO and Par=
tner at Nuage, on the back-to-basics test for AI=0Areadiness=0A=0ALouis Bal=
la [https://www.linkedin.com/in/louis-balla] is CRO and Partner at=0ANuage =
[https://www.nuagecg.com/about-us/], a consulting group working with=0Amanu=
facturers and distributors on process and technology decisions. At The=0AOp=
erations Brief's first roundtable in August, he pushed back on the instinct=
 to=0Aadd AI before the basics are settled.=0A=0A=E2=80=9CHave we thought t=
hrough our entire process, from design to build and order to=0Acash=3F Have=
 we ironed out the basics=3F Have we even mapped them out=3F=E2=80=9D=0A=0A=
Louis Balla=0A=0ACRO and Partner, Nuage [https://nuagecg.com] =C2=A0=C2=B7=
=C2=A0 The Operations Brief Roundtable=0A#1, August 2026=0A[https://docs.go=
ogle.com/document/d/1ZJBtVjgk98ONMXoEXs4YVqDHDxeijaJ81gyqjpHjBcw/edit]=0A=
=0AEarlier in the same conversation, Louis drew the line that matters: a te=
am can=0Agenerate a report in seconds and still struggle to synthesize it, =
align=0Aleadership, maintain segregation of duties, and decide what action =
follows.=0ARight after the quote above, he pointed to tribal knowledge and =
missing process=0Adocumentation as the real barriers to moving AI through a=
n organization, not the=0Atooling.=0A=0AThe takeaway=0A=0AA model can summa=
rize a process it can see. It cannot settle a process the=0Aorganization ha=
s never made explicit. The operating work is mapping the=0Adecisions, excep=
tions, and ownership before asking technology to accelerate=0Athem.=0A=0AMo=
re from the roundtable=0A=0A=C2=B7 Matt Gargas [https://www.linkedin.com/in=
/mattgargas/] of RDT=0A[https://rdt.com]: =E2=80=9CBroad AI rollouts design=
ed at the executive level often miss=0Athe day-to-day work. The useful scop=
e emerges when leaders walk the floor and=0Atalk with managers, engineers, =
operators, and quality staff about what should=0Aactually change.=E2=80=9D=
=0A=0A=C2=B7 Robert Wyse [https://www.linkedin.com/in/robert-wyse-3aa20884/=
] of=0AIndustronics Service Company [https://industronics.com/]: =E2=80=9CA=
utomate the=0Alower-risk, repetitive work so people can focus their attenti=
on on the=0Ahigher-risk decisions that cannot be allowed to slip.=E2=80=9D=
=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE STACK=0A=0A=0AThe Decision-Ready Process Map.=0A=0AA =
working specification for where AI may assist, and where people still decid=
e.=0A=0AOne document that turns a flowchart into an AI-readiness spec, befo=
re the pilot=0Astarts.=0A=0AMost process maps show activities and handoffs:=
 who does what, and where the=0Apaper or the ticket moves next. The Decisio=
n-Ready Process Map adds the layer an=0AAI-supported workflow actually need=
s: the source of truth, the decision being=0Amade, the exception that chang=
es the route, the evidence required, the=0Aaccountable owner, and the bound=
ary where human judgment has to stay in control.=0A=0AThe goal is not more =
documentation. It is one place to answer four questions:=0Awhat is the deci=
sion, what information is trusted, who owns the outcome, and=0Awhere could =
AI help without moving responsibility away from the person=0Aaccountable fo=
r the work.=0A=0AStart with one value stream where the pain is visible and =
measurable:=0Aquote-to-order, order-to-cash, supplier exception handling, i=
nventory=0Aprioritization, or quality disposition. Do not begin with an ent=
erprise-wide=0Amap.=0A=0AWhat goes into the map=0A=0A=C2=B7 Trigger and out=
come. What starts the process, and what business result proves=0Ait finishe=
d correctly.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Actual steps and systems. What people really do, i=
ncluding local spreadsheets,=0Ainboxes, sticky notes, and workarounds absen=
t from the official procedure.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Source of truth. Which field, re=
cord, or system is authoritative at each=0Acritical point.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Deci=
sion and owner. What judgment is being made, who is accountable, and who=0A=
has approval authority.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Required evidence. What the next person=
 must see before accepting the decision=0Aor handoff.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Exception=
s. What changes the normal route, who resolves it, where it=0Aescalates, an=
d what happens if it is missed.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Risk and consequence. Which err=
ors are reversible, and which affect safety,=0Aquality, customer commitment=
s, cash, or compliance.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Potential AI role. Could a model retrie=
ve, summarize, classify, detect,=0Apredict, recommend, or draft at this poi=
nt.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Human boundary and fallback. What requires human confirmati=
on, and how the=0Aprocess continues when the model or system is unavailable=
.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Baseline. Current cycle time, queue age, rework, error rate,=
 manual touches,=0Aand decision outcome.=0A=0AHow the team builds it=0A=0AE=
ight steps, one bounded process=0A=0A1. Select one bounded workflow and nam=
e its owner.=0A=0A2. Observe the real work with the people who perform it. =
Record workarounds=0Ainstead of copying the SOP.=0A=0A3. Map the normal pat=
h and the exceptions that change sequence, owner, evidence,=0Aor risk.=0A=
=0A4. At every decision point, name the authoritative input, accountable pe=
rson,=0Aallowed action, and required proof.=0A=0A5. Identify only the narro=
w steps where AI may assist.=0A=0A6. Define the model's limit, the human re=
view point, the escalation rule, and=0Athe non-AI fallback.=0A=0A7. Score w=
hether the process is ready based on agreement, source authority,=0Aexcepti=
on coverage, ownership, and an available baseline.=0A=0A8. Pilot the highes=
t-readiness assistive step. Add newly discovered exceptions=0Ato the map be=
fore expanding.=0A=0AWhat the operator gets=0A=0A=C2=B7 A current-state pro=
cess and decision map.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A source-of-truth table for critical inp=
uts.=0A=0A=C2=B7 An exception and workaround register.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A decisi=
on-rights and escalation matrix.=0A=0A=C2=B7 An AI-role map showing where a=
 model may assist and where it must not decide.=0A=0A=C2=B7 A readiness vie=
w showing what must be fixed before a pilot.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Baseline measures =
for cycle time, queue age, rework, error, and decision=0Aoutcomes.=0A=0ABui=
ld guardrails=0A=0A=C2=B7 Map actual work, not only the official procedure.=
=0A=0A=C2=B7 Keep high-consequence decisions under explicit human approval.=
=0A=0A=C2=B7 Give every AI output an evidence view and a non-AI fallback.=
=0A=0A=C2=B7 Version the map when systems, policy, ownership, or exceptions=
 change.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Update the process record with exceptions discovered d=
uring the pilot before=0Aexpanding automation.=0A=0A=C2=B7 Accept =E2=80=9C=
do not automate yet=E2=80=9D as a valid result when the source of truth,=0A=
ownership, or process agreement is unresolved.=0A=0ATwo paths=0A=0APath 1: =
Internal build=0A=0AStart with a shared document, whiteboard, or process-ma=
pping tool. Bring the=0Aprocess owner and two or three people who perform t=
he work into a 60-minute=0Asession. Map one real order, exception, or decis=
ion from trigger to completion.=0AAdd the fields above, then test the map a=
gainst the last three exceptions the=0Ateam handled.=0A=0APath 2: Existing =
technology partner=0A=0AAsk the ERP partner, systems integrator, or automat=
ion partner to document the=0Asame decision-ready fields before proposing a=
n AI workflow. The deliverable=0Ashould show source systems, decision owner=
s, exception routes, evidence=0Arequirements, model boundaries, fallback, a=
nd baseline measures. The operating=0Ateam should own the process map even =
if the partner builds the integration.=0A=0AIf the process is not clear eno=
ugh for a new employee to follow, it is not clear=0Aenough for AI to improv=
e.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0ATHE OPERATIONS BRIEF=0A=0ABy Bill Murphy =C2=A0=C2=B7=
=C2=A0 Powered by Colony Spark [https://www.colonyspark.com/]=0A=0AAlready =
mapping decisions before the AI conversation starts, or watching a=0Arollou=
t stall on process gaps nobody has agreed on=3F Hit reply, I read every one=
.=0ABill
